  "worked_solution": "The rotation matrix ${R}_{\\theta }$ represents a rotation of angle $\\theta $ counterclockwise about the origin and is given by\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta  & -\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta  & \\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta \\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nComparing $A$ with the rotation matrix, we have \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince both $\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta $ and $\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta $ are positive, we must have $0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}$ \n\nDividing the first equation by the second gives\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}\u2061\\theta =1.\n$$\n\nThe principal value of ${\\theta }_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{1}=\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061(1)=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A$ represents a rotation of $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ radians counterclockwise about the origin.",
